      "sourceLink": null,
      "spam": false,
      "statusChangeID": null,
      "value": "For anyone saying the home IP tracking will prevent sold accounts, are not aware how the internet and WAN IPs work.\n\nI work rural IT support. Not with any big company. I do house calls to SMBs (Small Medium Businesses), and to homes. I see a variety of users, issues, and environments.\n\nVast majority of Internet Subscribers, do not have a Static IP. A Static IP is an IP assigned to you, that should never change. I say Should, some scenarios come up, and some ISPs manage this different...it's a headache.\n\nBack on the simple track... Homes very rarely keep the same IP. Most times, the IP's last segment will rotate, sometimes the whole thing. Sometimes once a month, quarterly, or weekly pending how often the modem is restarted.\n\nI have two ISPs in my town area, your IP might start with (throwing generic numbers to explain) 108.x.x.x, then next month be 64.x.x.x. When I do speed tests, half the time it's my local town appearing, sometimes it's the capital, or the next state over. I live in Oklahoma, on Optimum (aka Suddenlink back when), many times I come up as on Altice instead of Optimum, sometimes I come up in Texas where Suddenlink's headquarters was. Then you toss in users who use a VPN.\n\nThis is why Real Perm Bans by IP is no longer \"as effective\" as it used to be. Either a restart of your modem/router (pending equipment and ISP), contacting your ISP, or for those advanced enough to know how to, masking your MAC Address forcing a new IP.",
      "viewerReactions": null,
      "voteID": null
    },
