      "sourceFeatureExtractionItemID": null,
      "sourceLink": null,
      "spam": false,
      "statusChangeID": null,
      "value": "Frankly speaking, that sort of crime is commonplace. It’s no different from kids stealing their parents' alcohol or begging older people for cigarettes—and adults actually giving it to them. Unless you handcuff everyone and keep them under 24-hour surveillance, people are bound to commit crimes or get caught up in them. Ultimately, wrongdoers keep their misdeeds quiet, and acts committed in secret cannot be reported. No matter how many laws you enact, a country where the police and parents fail to step up is never going to produce rational children; without the foundation of proper upbringing, the system itself is meaningless. True justice isn't about striking a heroic pose while championing a system that is impossible to enforce. I’m not saying we should just turn a blind eye, of course, but going to such extremes only leaves a lot of people feeling resentful, while the bad guys simply carry on with their misdeeds quietly.\n\nLet’s focus on what’s actually feasible. Teach others to make friends who follow the rules and to avoid siding with wrongdoers. Or, at the very least, don’t raise people who would lend a hand to such things.\n\nWell, I’ll let you in on a little secret: the real problem lies with the laws and the state for shifting the responsibility for wrongdoing onto the platforms.",
